     "tests_redelivery_settles": "that every alarm reaches a human — it proves the QUEUE stops asking once one has, which is a different guarantee. Four blind spots: (a) it settles on an explicit key, so a redelivery path that forgets to pass `settles` regresses silently and only the repeat count would show it, days later; (b) it does not retroactively settle the six already sent — the seventh redelivery closes the loop, so the ledger keeps six rows that look like a live defect; (c) `SUPPRESSED` counts as settled, which is right for a test harness and wrong if anyone ever suppresses in production; (d) it says nothing about whether the redelivered TEXT is still meaningful — a stale alarm replayed hours later is delivered, settled, and possibly misleading",
     "tests_alarm_digest": "that the right things get read — it proves the classifier separates and never demotes an undelivered alarm, not that the tiers match what a human would choose. Four blind spots: (a) the rules are REGEX over message text, so rewording an alarm silently reclassifies it and nothing here would notice; (b) `UNRECOGNISED -> DECIDE` is safe but means the tool degrades toward noise exactly as the system produces novel shapes — i.e. during an incident; (c) tier B asserts 'already decided', which is a claim about a RULING that lives outside the code — if a ruling is reversed, the tier is silently wrong; (d) it measures compression on the last 24h and reports the historical ratio ungraded, so a slow drift toward everything-is-A across weeks would not fail anything",
-    "tests_disposition_matrix": "that no name will evaporate again — it proves every terminal state HAS a declared destination and that the ledger sizes the ones we abandon, not that the destinations are the right ones. Four blind spots: (a) the matrix is closed over `TERMINAL_REASONS`, so a leg that ends WITHOUT writing a row at all is invisible to it — and that is precisely how -5022 vanished, by never reaching a disposition; (b) it is RECORD-ONLY, so a gap being named changes nothing about whether a guard forgives it — the ledger could be perfect and §4-5e would still flatten the book; (c) the two code-dependent cells resolve on a substring of `note`, so a venue that reworded its errors would silently reclassify -5022 as a gap and -4164 as a real one; (d) `blocked_by_halt` dominating a halted anchor at ~4200 USDT is arithmetically right and operationally noisy — nothing here establishes that a halted anchor SHOULD report its whole book as gap",
+    "tests_disposition_matrix": "that no name will evaporate again — it proves every terminal state HAS a declared destination and that the ledger sizes the ones we abandon, not that the destinations are the right ones. Four blind spots: (a) the matrix is closed over `TERMINAL_REASONS`, so a leg that ends WITHOUT writing a row at all is invisible to it — and that is precisely how -5022 vanished, by never reaching a disposition; (b) it is RECORD-ONLY, so a gap being named changes nothing about whether a guard forgives it — the ledger could be perfect and §4-5e would still flatten the book; (c) the two code-dependent cells resolve on a substring of `note`, so a venue that reworded its errors would silently reclassify -5022 as a gap and -4164 as a real one; (d) `blocked_by_halt` dominating a halted anchor at ~4200 USDT is arithmetically right and operationally noisy — nothing here establishes that a halted anchor SHOULD report its whole book as gap"
+        " E3 (FX-EXEC 2026-09-13): the watchdog event log is read line by line; a record that is not the writer's shape (a top-level"
+        " JSON array / scalar / null, non-JSON, a bad ts, non-list actions or orders, a non-object _exec, a key that cannot be read)"
+        " is NAMED NOT OBSERVABLE and fails the completeness cell instead of ending the suite by exception or being skipped. It does"
+        " NOT recover what such a line said: a flatten record lost to corruption with its ledger rows also gone is named only as an"
+        " unreadable line, never reconstructed, and a log that loses whole lines on disk (no line left to read) is still invisible. E5 (FX-EXEC 2026-09-13): the ruler's `_clamp_known` / `_row_rejudged` apply the same single-comparison and exact-copy rules in-body, with parity against reconcile on every E5 shape",
     "tests_reject_topup": "that the book will reach its target — it recovers ONE refusal class (-5022) and makes the rest audible, and those are different guarantees. Four blind spots: (a) the recovered residual is now taken as a TAKER at a moment the market has just moved THROUGH our resting price, which is by definition the expensive moment — this suite asserts the order exists, never that the price was good, and the cost of the fix is unmeasured here; (b) -1111/-4164/-1102 clusters can still open the identical hole and by design cannot be auto-retried, so the only thing between them and another 4-5e flatten is an alarm a human must read; (c) it does not establish that 5% is the right alarm threshold — one anchor at 15.6% is not a distribution; (d) the top-up itself can fail, and a refused top-up of a refused maker leaves the same hole with two rows instead of none — better forensics, same exposure",
     "tests_ops_flag_safety": "that an operator cannot change production state by accident — it closes ONE tool's flag handling and enforces the class for shell scripts only. Four blind spots: (a) the closed-world scan covers `ops/*.sh` and NOT the python tools, which are safe today only because they happen to use argparse — a future `sys.argv[1]` in a state-changing python tool would not be caught here; (b) it cannot see the inverse error, a flag that IS recognised but does the wrong thing; (c) `KILL.sh` reads no arguments at all, so `KILL.sh --check` silently fires the real kill — for an emergency stop firing is the safe direction, so it is left alone, but the expectation gap that caused this incident is still present there and this suite does not close it; (d) it asserts refusal, not comprehension: nothing here checks that the usage text an operator then reads is accurate",
     "tests_exit_ledger": "that every position change will be explicable — it closes ONE unrecorded path (the universe exit) and proves that path end to end, from the emitted row to the real reconciler. It does not enumerate the others: any future code that moves the book without writing an order row reproduces 2026-08-01 exactly, and nothing here would notice. Three further blind spots: (a) it asserts the row EXPLAINS the change, not that the exit was the RIGHT action — a wrongly-classified name would be flattened and cleanly explained, and this suite would be green; (b) the fills.jsonl half is reachable only because the exit now carries a client id, but venue-side attribution itself is `venue_fills`' job and is not exercised here — an id that the venue never echoes back would still leave fills unattributed; (c) the fill facts come from `last_fill_details()` read one order at a time, so a broker that returned a stale answer would be recorded faithfully and wrongly, and no assertion here can tell those apart",
@@ -211,7 +256,14 @@ SUITE_SCOPE = {
     "tests_venue_rate_authority": "that the 80% backstop is ENOUGH — we still do not know what banned us on 07-30, only that it was not any counter we kept. The backstop makes the venue's own number authoritative and the out-of-band gap measurable; whether 80% of a published weight limit is the binding constraint at all is a question the next incident answers, and this time the record will contain the answer",
     "tests_venue_cap_clamp": "that the venue's cap is the number symbolConfig reports — it proves the pure clamp (shrink-only, sign-preserving, cap 0 and missing config untouched, over-cap positions reduced to the limit, PIEVERSE replica) and the wiring order pop→reshape→held-clamp→cap-clamp→plan. It cannot see whether the venue evaluates -2027 on mark price or on order price (the 2% margin is a policy number, not a measurement), nor whether a cap changes between arm and submit, nor whether the book's net shift from the clamp is worth hedging",
     "tests_request_identity_unknown": "that the venue will ever produce these sequences — every outcome is injected. It proves, against the real submit/requote/top-up/attribution/reconcile code, that a -2013×2 'absent' is carried as ambiguous and never re-sent this anchor, that every venue request has its own client id (-1/-2/-3/-3cN) and the resolver refuses a record under another id, that an UNKNOWN request survives a later refusal and a partial attribution, that reconcile consumes the unknown as an authorised band instead of a hole, that 408/-1007/-1006 are execution-unknown, and that the cap clamp validates and applies atomically; round 4 (review 62ebf9f8): that the top-up keeps a per-request ledger in which a rejected or never-sent request contributes NOTHING to the unknown bound, that the known part keeps its sign when child fills settle it, that attribution shrinks the unknown by request identity (orderId join) and closes the leg only when no request is left unknown, that reconcile sums per-request QUANTITY intervals (Minkowski, mixed directions do not cancel, mark-independent) and treats non-finite or side-less rows as unquantifiable, that an ambiguous maker absent from a FULL allOrders page stays UNKNOWN and is settled only by its own order query (-2013 ⇒ absent), that an UNKNOWN symbol gets a bounded maker row instead of a hole, that flatten retries mint distinct ids, that legs are keyed by (symbol, orderId), and that an id over 36 chars is refused rather than truncated. round 5 (review cfaf1bbe): that the row's quantity comes from each request's own executedQty on every path (two prices ⇒ 100, never 150/2), that a child fill raises a request's confirmed quantity without ending it (band = what it may still fill; the leg closes only when every request is terminal), that contradictions (over-fill, wrong side, non-finite) are checked before any classification and keep the leg open, that child fills are de-duplicated by trade id and confirmed quantity is monotone, that an order the venue shows OPEN is cancelled and re-queried and stays UNKNOWN if still open, that cancel-unresolved symbols are UNKNOWN, that the breaker records the maker leg before refusing the top-up, that target/held are validated for every name before the cap logic, and that flatten recovery keys by (symbol, orderId) with a process-wide id counter. It does NOT prove phase B's allOrders/order reads (mocked here) resolve every leg on the real venue, nor that the venue's -2013 after the k-window is final (a stated venue-consistency assumption), nor that two PROCESSES in the same UTC second cannot mint the same flatten id, nor that the latest-only reconcile baseline carries an unexplained residual forward (registered for pre-registration, not implemented). Round 6 (review ce7b3cf4): that a later terminal fact for a request (cancel + re-query in settlement) supersedes the k-cancel's unresolved set by request and clears its pin, that a terminal request with a trusted executed quantity has no remainder whether or not its USDT amount was readable (C = Q with an unknown amount is a KNOWN quantity), that a missing executedQty is not a measured zero, that a ledger row is quantified by its ledger only (no amount-over-subset-price fallback), that the bounded reader validates its price, that a conflicting duplicate trade id is a contradiction, that an open order's executed-so-far reaches the row when its cancel fails, and that the pre-flight uses the plan's real chunk count. Round 7 (review a99c447e, from the fact table docs/DESIGN_request_fact_model): that the ORIGINAL complete_anchor chain (real broker over a scripted transport → cancel → allOrders → order query → top-up → attribution → rows) keeps a readable executedQty when the amount is unreadable (CANCELED 4 ⇒ filled_qty 4, position 10 ⇒ anomaly 6), that three explicit refusals at an unchanged position (0 and 1) leave a determined filled_qty 0 and no anomaly (the round-6 false trigger), that the decoder and the allOrders fold record contradictions (negative executedQty, explicit 0 beside a positive amount) instead of zeros, that an allOrders-terminal request resolves the k-cancel's unresolved set and pin, and that the ledger reader owns the row's columns at emission (a branch cannot clear them). Round 8 (review 82cbe018): that the submit response and the amount re-query are MERGED per field (a record lacking executedQty never revokes an earlier 20; an explicit terminal 0 read at the GET entry is a measured zero; a falling executedQty or NEW after terminal is a contradiction), that an allOrders match resolves the k-cancel's unresolved set only with an explicit terminal status AND our identity (no status ⇒ exact query; origQty/symbol/side mismatch ⇒ UNKNOWN), that a fold contradiction makes the name UNKNOWN end to end (no top-up, reader refuses), and that with a ledger the row's amount and price are the reader's (an unclosed ledger writes None and relabels). It does NOT run phase C or the watchdog itself (reconcile is called on the emitted rows), its venue is FakeNet. Round 9 (review b92e9479, DESIGN §3c): that a derived quantity or amount comes from ONE record (an amount 40 in one snapshot and a price 3 in another derive nothing — C unknown, 40 a lower bound, the row a band), that a terminal executedQty is CONSTANT (20 then 30 is a contradiction end to end; a terminal record without a C may still learn it; child fills beyond a terminal C are a contradiction), that the k-cancel's DELETE body is a record of the request (CANCELED 4 survives a sparse terminal query ⇒ top-up 6; a differing terminal 6 ⇒ contradiction, no top-up; an unreached symbol's UNKNOWN row carries the 4; -2013 after a cancel with facts is a contradiction, not absent), that a sparse terminal record (no executedQty, no cumQuote) is unreadable rather than 0, that the identity gate governs FACTS (a record failing it makes the request unmeasurable instead of entering the ledger as C 4 / terminal; a missing side, a non-finite origQty and an orderId other than the acknowledged one fail it; a side-less fill is never read as SELL), that contradictions travel with the facts on the partial path (query and page agree), and that the amount re-query's record must be OUR request (client id / symbol / orderId / origQty / side compared when the record carries them; a mismatch makes the request unmeasurable). Round 10 (review 38808cbd, DESIGN §3d): that a quantity carries its COMPLETENESS TYPE (an open snapshot's 20 followed by a terminal record without a quantity is a lower bound — [20, Q], known 70 / band 30 beside a second leg of 50, legal 70–100 CLEAN, 60 and 110 anomalies — while a terminal snapshot's 20 is final; a child-fill subset raises the lower bound and never proves the total; a child set that reaches Q does), that every UNKNOWN exit first folds the records already held (a CANCELED 4 survives an exhausted or -2013 query — the latter as a contradiction; a settlement cancel that answered CANCELED 6 survives a failed re-query; the submit response's own PARTIALLY_FILLED 4 is the first record; a status-only DELETE body proves existence and terminality, so -2013 after it is a contradiction, not absent), and that two final snapshots with different amounts contradict. Round 11 (review f79dc8cd, DESIGN §3e): that a final amount is never divided by a lower-bound quantity to make a price, that a maker leg the venue told us about carries a request ledger so its quantity is the reader's band (open 4 / final amount 6 ⇒ known 4 / band 6, total [8, 14] beside a top-up of 4), that a non-object query answer still folds the records already held, that a child subset's amount is a lower bound (the amount closes only with the set; reconcile reads a ledger row's band whether or not its amount closed), that a missing or None finality key is NOT final (no legacy fallback) while a lower bound reaching Q is final by capacity, that a top-up response without a status proves no finality, and that the 